new file mode 100644
index 00000000..3bc666d9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/government/APPOINTMENT-RECOVERY.md
@@ -0,0 +1,45 @@
+# Terminal appointment recovery — Act 19 implementation note
+
+`appointments.sh ensure` is the serialization point for changing the durable
+logical-office → disposable-agent mapping. Before it replaces a terminal or
+unreadable ID, it persists the exact status response, bounded log tail,
+worktree HEAD and dirtiness under
+`$VICHE_STATE_ROOT/appointments/terminal-evidence/AGENT_ID/`. Its JSONL index
+is repaired idempotently if a prior process stopped after publishing the
+snapshot but before indexing it. Each invocation identity has its own
+`AGENT_ID/INVOCATION_ID/` snapshot, so a later failed run by the same Antonina
+ID cannot be hidden by an earlier terminal observation. Evidence failure
+blocks replacement.
+
+For a failed transient invocation, the same locked path appends one office
+event keyed by **(agent ID, invocation ID or finished identity)**. Repeated
+polling is idempotent; events survive replacement and are never truncated.
+Successful invocations and commits break the consecutive streak. The third
+distinct transient failure in 1800 seconds claims the Act 19 reset, subject to
+the 1800-second office cooldown and current-worktree-HEAD progress guard.
+`recoveries.log` and `last-reset-KEY` are written before the one replacement.
+
+**Interpretation of Act 19 §3.1.** An Antonina terminal agent cannot continue
+its session. Reappointing a usable session after preserving/classifying that
+terminal invocation is treated as operational continuity, not as the Act's
+failure-count reset. The §3.1 reset is separately recorded and is not claimed
+until the distinct-event threshold and guards pass. This reading preserves
+both terminal-agent safety and the enacted reset detector. If the Legislature
+intended every terminal reappointment itself to be prohibited before the
+third failure, the incompatible text is Act 19 §3.1, “are reset after a
+threshold of consecutive transient failures”; that intent is not explicit in
+the enacted sentence and would require legislative clarification, not an
+Executive code reinterpretation.
+
+`automatic_retry_safe=false` (and missing retry-safety evidence) creates a
+durable per-office redispatch hold. The appointment may be replaced, but the
+failed operation is not automatically rerun; the open durable handoff remains
+queued. Permanent authentication/configuration failures are surfaced and do
+not trigger appointment replacement. Interrupted sessions are captured and
+held unless explicitly classified safe. No model or variant is selected or
+checked by Viche.
+
+**Remaining operational limitation:** unsafe holds intentionally have no
+automatic expiry. They require review before dispatch can resume; silently
+replaying the handoff would violate the runtime retry classification. This
+implementation does not add an automated hold-release interface.
